course: dictionary
lesson: 254
title: Alright: many meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Alright» has several meanings, so the words around it tell you which one you need. It can mean fine, safe or not hurt: «Are you alright?» asks if someone is hurt or ill.
explain: It can also mean good enough or OK: «It's alright» means it is acceptable, not great. Or it means yes, OK: «Alright, let's go» agrees to something. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence before you decide.
explain: In the UK and Ireland, «Alright?» can be a greeting, like saying hello. A bus driver who says «You alright, son?» to a crying boy is asking how he really is, so check the situation before you choose the meaning.
fact: Alright is an adjective, an adverb and an interjection, so it can work in different places in a sentence.
fact: «Alright» can mean fine, safe, or not hurt.
fact: «Alright» can mean good enough, OK, or yes, used to agree.
fact: In the UK and Ireland, «Alright?» is a greeting that means hello, how are you.
fact: Alright is another spelling of all right, so the two mean the same thing.
exercise: «Are you alright, Ella?» she asked after the fall. What does alright mean here? | *Not hurt, fine | Lost and confused | Very tired and sleepy | Ready to start the race
exercise: Mum says «Alright, let's go» when you ask to leave the park. What does alright mean? | *Yes, OK | Stop right now | Angry and cross | Wrong
exercise: Someone in Bristol says «Alright my lover» to you in the street. What is it? | A shout for help | A question about the weather | *A friendly greeting said to anyone | A rude insult
test: «The girls did alright today» after a 5–0 cup final win. What does it really mean? | They lost the match | *They were brilliant, even though it sounds modest | They were just average and forgettable | They never played
test: The driver asks Jake, «You alright, son?» What is the driver really asking? | Whether Jake has paid his fare | Whether the bus is late | *Whether Jake is OK and not upset | Whether Jake wants a sweet
exam: Mum says «It'll be alright» after the storm, when you are scared. What does she mean? | Nobody is coming to help | It is a rule you must obey | *It will turn out fine in the end | It will rain all week
